 "briefing": "# Stripe Briefing: Changes from 16 October to 15 November 2026\n\n## Research Status\n\nNo verified Stripe items dated 16 October to 15 November 2026 were found. Every search attempt failed with \"Rate limit exceeded,\" so no articles, press releases, changelog entries, or other pages were retrieved. The brief requires a dated source URL for every fact, so this briefing contains no factual claims about the window.\n\nThe empty results should not be read as evidence that nothing happened. The sources were not reached, so the research is incomplete in every category. Before the meeting, the primary sources below should be checked directly and filtered to the window.\n\n## News and Launches\n\nNo product launches, feature releases, API or pricing changes, regulatory or legal matters, or partnership announcements were verified for the window. Stripe's primary sources that should be checked first are:\n\n- Stripe's newsroom for product launches, partnerships, and company announcements [1]\n- Stripe's blog for product and engineering announcements [2]\n- Stripe's API changelog for API version changes, deprecations, and pricing-related API updates [3]\n\nRegulatory and legal news was not checked against any source. Reuters, Bloomberg, the Financial Times, The Wall Street Journal, and TechCrunch would be the next places to search, limited to 16 October to 15 November 2026.\n\n## Hires\n\nNo executive appointments, senior departures, board changes, or notable hiring announcements were verified for the window. Stripe's newsroom [1] and blog [2] are the primary places to check for leadership announcements. Business press coverage and LinkedIn announcements cited in the press should also be checked, since senior departures are often reported there first.\n\n## Deals\n\nNo acquisitions, investments, partnerships, customer wins, funding rounds, tender offers, or other financing events were verified for the window. Stripe's newsroom [1] should be checked first for partnership and customer announcements. Funding, valuation, and tender-offer reporting should be checked in Crunchbase News, PitchBook, and the business outlets listed above, with each result's date confirmed against the window.\n\n## Verification Steps Before 17 November\n\n1. Review Stripe's newsroom [1], blog [2], and API changelog [3], filtering each for items dated 16 October to 15 November 2026.\n2. Run separate searches for each category (launches, API or pricing, legal, hires, and deals) and record the URL and date of every item found.\n3. Rank items by materiality and cut the list to fit one page.\n4. Re-run the search if the rate limit has cleared, and treat any category with no dated, sourced items as \"no qualifying changes found\" only after the primary sources have been checked.\n\n## Open Questions for the Meeting\n\n1. Which product launches, API changes, or pricing updates did Stripe ship in the past month, and where are they documented for customers?\n2. Have there been any changes in Stripe's senior leadership or board recently, and how are those roles being filled?\n3. Which partnerships, acquisitions, or funding arrangements has Stripe completed or announced recently, and how do they affect the areas you are focused on?\n\n### Sources\n\n[1] Stripe Newsroom: https://stripe.com/newsroom\n[2] Stripe Blog: https://stripe.com/blog\n[3] Stripe API Changelog: https://docs.stripe.com/changelog",
